awplus# show interface port1.0.16 status
    Port        Name               Status      Vlan      Duplex Speed   Type
    port1.0.2   -                  connected           1 a-full a-1000  1000BASE-T
    port1.0.16  -                  connected           1 a-full a-1000  1000BASE-T

awplus# show spanning-tree brief
    Default: Bridge up - Spanning Tree Enabled
    Default: Root Path Cost 20000 - Root Port 5002 -  Bridge Priority 32768
    Default: Root Id 8000:0000cd423029
    Default: Bridge Id 8000:e01aead47043
    port1.0.2       8000:0000cd423029    838a       Rootport         Forwarding
    port1.0.16      8000:0000cd423029    8398       Alternate        Discarding

  Both ports are up.  Both reach the same far-end bridge, 8000:0000cd423029, which is swi_b and
  is also the STP root.  That is what makes them a matched pair.
  port1.0.16 is Discarding under RSTP.  That does not matter for this case.  An RSTP role
  decides whether a port forwards user traffic.  It does not decide whether the physical link
  state changes.  This case measures physical link state.  STEP 5 proves the point directly:
  the DUT logs port1.0.16's down and up events exactly as it logs port1.0.2's.

  `show running-config interface port1.0.2` and `... port1.0.16` are three lines each at
  baseline (`switchport`, `switchport mode access`).  Neither carries `snmp trap link-status`.
  => STEP 1 PASS: the two test ports are a matched pair.  Same model, same DUT, same partner
     switch, same media, both connected, and neither has any per-port trap configuration.

